i started my first official job and by  that i mean a job where i wasn't getting  paid cash under the table but actually  had to fill out paperwork and was  getting paid through payroll when i was  15 years old and it started in the  summer  of  1986 and the title that i had  was teen peer educator which is a bit of  a mouthful and we'll talk about what it  meant  in just a minute but i'd like to sort of  sketch this out for you so you know it  was the summer in between sophomore and  junior year and i already was making  money you know cutting grass and stuff  like that but my mom said you should try  to find a job and you know i wasn't  doing an awful lot over the summer  anyway and i did want to have some more  money so i got out the  waukesha freeman and i started looking  in the want ad section where jobs were  being advertised and one that i saw was  for teen peer educator and it you know  it was for a job in waukesha at the  ywca that's the young woman's  christian association it's sort of like  the ymca only  different gender  and it said that you know they're paying  a decent uh amount and that there'd be  training and stuff like that  so i think i applied for some other jobs  but didn't hear back from them and i  applied  for this one and i if i recall correctly  i had to go into waukesha go to the ywca  fill out the application form i believe  there was an interview of some sort  although it's you know my recollection  is pretty dim  about that because i remember so many  other aspects of this interesting  work  and so you know i was told i probably  got a phone call or something  yep you're in and training is going to  begin  on such and such a day  and the training lasted i think  either three or four weeks and it was  every day you know monday  through friday  it would begin in the morning i think  around nine o'clock and then they would  they would give us lunch  and then you know we'd have the  afternoon and you know that was all of  what we were doing for that entire  space of time  and i would live i didn't live in  waukesha i lived way out in uh wales uh  in in the subdivision called the hills  of delafield and so i would each morning  get my 10-speed bike  and i would get onto the gracial drumlin  trail which is now very nicely paved and  runs you know a long ways it's it's old  train tracks that were ripped up and  turned into bike paths and it goes you  know i think way past madison and now i  think past waukesha at that time  the trail  ended in waukesha on the west side  so i would take it all ready to go visit  my friends in waukesha it wasn't a big  deal for me it was probably 10 miles or  so to go so i would get on the trail in  wales itself  and then  bike out and you know took me anywhere  from 45 minutes to an hour because you  know you get to waukesha you get off the  trail and then you had another couple  miles to bike into the  ywca as well and so then you know park  my bike lock it up go in  and then we  had these classes and the classes were  very interesting  because what the teen peer educator  program was about  ultimately  was  responding to the incredibly high rates  of teen pregnancy in waukesha county now  if you don't know anything about  waukesha county  back then just as much as it is today it  was the republican capital  of wisconsin we used to joke about it  being such that that's where a lot of  the power structure was and a lot of the  people there  you know were sort of  living out a republican ideology which  back at that time meant uh don't have  any premarital sex don't use  contraceptives  uh you know anti-abortion all that sort  of stuff and as you can guess you know  you take a bunch of horny teenagers  and none of that actually works and so  you know pregnancy rates were quite high  uh as a matter of fact uh we even when  we moved to waukesha a couple years  later um  from from wales the the girl next door  to us actually got pregnant and had to  go to a special school to finish up her  high school so it was a significant  problem  and the idea was here's where the teen  pier comes in  they're not listening to adults these  middle schoolers and high schoolers  what if we took  kids you know high school kids  you know from 15 on up not freshmen  and we gave them some training not just  in you know sexuality matters health  stuff but also  human psychology and relationships and  then we had them go in and like lead  exercises and talk to these kids they  would listen to these kids because  they're more relatable their peers right  and no that didn't actually turn out to  be the case but they were willing to  throw money at it there were grants  available and so they had this really  cool program and so for i think you know  three or four weeks that's what we did  we had like textbooks and we learned all  sorts of things about human sexuality  like we went through every single topic  that was you know discussable at the  time obviously we weren't like learning  about every single possible sexual  combination or anything like that but  we're learning a lot more than we would  typically get  in a health class especially in  a uh county that was run by republicans  where the  the uh education on that was more or  less keep your legs together don't have  sex uh if you do somebody's gonna get  pregnant and then it'll ruin your life  right so we learned about contraceptives  we also learned quite a bit about a  number of other things including  drugs and alcohol  you know some public speaking things we  did a lot of role playing and we learned  what sort of exercises we could lead  these kids through for example  one of the most popular exercises was to  go in  and then i would be with the boys and  you know i i would be paired up with a  female teen peer educator who would be  there with the girls and each of us  would have like you know one of those  big pads of paper and we'd say tell us  about you know what you're really  looking for in a partner what makes them  attractive what makes them sexy what  makes you know for girlfriend material  we'd go down the line and pretty soon  they'd say things like you know  attractive body and all that and then  we'd say okay we've got all these  different factors now they get in a  horrible accident and their body is just  ruined so we start crossing things off  you know and then we'd say what do you  have left would you still stay with them  if they weren't as attractive anymore if  they couldn't have sex with you anymore  and of course you know the proper  response that they would give is oh well  i would stay with them anyway because of  their lovely personality or their  loyalty or stuff like that and we knew  that that probably was not the case  anymore for these kids than it was for  their parents you know um who were  many that were getting divorced or you  know remarrying or cheating or things  like that  and  um  anyway we we did this whole education  thing so like from you know nine in the  morning till  three in the afternoon  and then we'd be cut loose and then i'd  ride my bike home to wales oftentimes i  would stop at le dukes which is a  custard stand that's still there  get myself a couple hot dogs you know  actually chili dogs and maybe a malt or  something like that and then  head home and what was cool about this  was  i was getting paid to go to these these  sessions so i was getting paid to learn  things and then once the program  actually kicked off  we would get to like leave school and go  to other schools and lead these  exercises and give talks and stuff like  that so it was it was fun to like  actually go somewhere else  interestingly enough  some of the  some of the young men and women in the  program  were actually hooking up with each other  and  you know so it wasn't that  effective of a program i think  and they were from all school schools  all over the waukesha uh county area so  you know they're  i went to catholic memorial and there  were like kids from some of the other  waukesha there was north and south at  the time and i think there was somebody  from mukwonago and people from  brookfield and all different schools  and you know so you'd get to meet a lot  of other kids and  talk about all these sexuality matters  and it was kind of good for me to to get  to know  things that um  weren't in our health curriculum  i i don't think that there were that  many things in the sex ed component that  i hadn't at least heard of  or wasn't aware of but you know it was a  little bit more rigorous study but then  there was all the other cool stuff about  like public speaking and you know drugs  and alcohol and you know whatever else  relationship matters  um  human psychology that was that was quite  useful for me  as well and so i did this for the next  two years  while i was in my junior and senior year  of high school and it was you know a  nice supplemental income  on the side i don't think i did it um  that much  my senior year  but um you know it was kind of cool to  go in the program was entirely  ineffective it did not move the needle  at all on teen pregnancy rates  it doesn't appear to have been  particularly  useful for the schools a lot of money  relatively speaking it's not like you  know the kind of money that goes into  building roads or something like that  but a grant  got thrown at this and i benefited from  some of it and you know maybe some of  the other um not not just the other teen  peer educators and not just the the  people running the thing but maybe some  of the other kids  in uh these schools where we were going  in benefited from it but there was no  tangible  benefit that we could actually point to  and you know fairly shortly after that  the whole thing was  ended i think by my by the end of my  senior year  and i don't know if they've tried other  things like this but that was for me  one of the  first jobs that i i had where i was  actually you know getting paid in a  proper way you could say  becoming part of the vast system  that you know we we have  in terms of taxes and finances and uh  things you can put on your resume i  think i've long since left it off my  resume  but it was kind of a cool thing  for a 15 year old and then you know 16  and 17 year old kid to get to do  and  you know maybe  even though it was a flop as far as  programs go it was a good thing overall  to try so that is the story  of my  first on paper job as a teen peer  educator for the ywca of waukesha county  you
 

